
  AutoHelix v0.1.1
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Goal       Make `kernel.neff` execute faster on the device without changing
             what it computes. `kernel.neff` is the compiled NEFF of the nkilib
             GDN chunked prefill kernel (`nkilib.experimental.gdn.gdn_cte`) for
             batch 32, seq_len 2304, head_dim 128, bfloat16, on one logical
             NeuronCore (LNC=1), one rank. It is the slowest case in the GDN
             benchmark, so it has the most to gain. The unedited NEFF and its
             measurements are in `baseline/`; measure the current one yourself
             rather than trusting any number written down here. THE ONLY FILE
             YOU MAY CHANGE IS `kernel.neff`. There is no kernel source in this
             repo and recompiling one is not the task: the work is to edit the
             compiled binary. Every other file (tools/, bench/, baseline/) is
             reverted before your iteration is judged. ## How to edit a NEFF
             Everything you need is documented in
             `/home/ubuntu/workspace/neff_doc` -- read it, do not guess. Start
             with its `README.md`, then: chapter 14 optimization playbook: where
             time goes, which levers exist, which were tested chapter 13 editing
             guide: the modify/run/check/measure procedure and a worked example
             chapter 10 synchronisation: the semaphore model and the rules an
             edit must respect chapter 6 the 64-byte instruction slot; chapter 7
             what each instruction's fields mean chapter 9 DMA (queued and DGE);
             chapter 5 SBUF/PSUM addressing chapter 15 the tools, with what each
             one is for The tools live in
             `/home/ubuntu/workspace/neff_doc/tools/`. Use them; they are
             tested: neff.py unpack kernel.neff work/ unpack
             (payload/sg00/<Engine>0.bin are the streams) isa.py
             work/payload/sg00/Pool0.bin decode every instruction slot, by
             vendor field name semaphores.py work sg00 check no wait can exceed
             its supply (catches hangs) neff.py pack work/ kernel.neff repack
             (recomputes the header size and both MD5s) neff_diff.py
             baseline/kernel.neff kernel.neff see exactly which bytes/fields you
             changed Run them with `/home/ubuntu/workspace/.venv/bin/python`. ##
             The loop's rules * `tools/verify.py` must pass: the NEFF runs on
             the device with fixed inputs and every output must stay within
             `max_abs_err <= 8e-4` of the torch golden. That is far tighter than
             the upstream test's 5e-2, and the unedited NEFF already spends most
             of the budget on `output_1` (`baseline/verify_baseline.json`; the
             verifier prints how much of the limit each output uses), so edits
             that change the arithmetic (narrower dtypes, dropped terms,
             reordered accumulation) will fail. Reordering, re-scheduling and
             removing redundant work are the ways to win. *
             `tools/single_core.py` must pass: still one subgraph (LNC=1), no
             collectives, no replica groups, same input names and output count.
             Spreading the work over 2 physical cores or 4 ranks is not a fair
             comparison against the shipped kernel, so it is rejected. * The
             metric is `total_exec_time_us` from `tools/profile.py` (median of 3
             captures) and LOWER IS BETTER. An iteration that regresses it by
             more than 2% is rejected. ## Finding where the time goes Measure
             the NEFF you are holding; do not work from remembered numbers, they
             go stale as the loop changes the NEFF. `tools/profile.py` prints
             the per-engine busy time and writes `profile_latest.json`;
             `baseline/profile_baseline.json` is the unedited NEFF for
             comparison. Read it like this: if one engine is busy for nearly the
             whole execution, that engine is the limit and the lever is to give
             it less work or move work off it. If no engine is near 100%, the
             kernel is losing time to serialisation rather than throughput, so
             look for waits stricter than the data dependency requires, work
             that could start earlier, and gaps where every engine is idle.
             `tools/profile.py --parquet prof` ingests the profile into
             `prof/Instruction.parquet` (`engine`, `pcore_idx`, `compiler_pc`,
             `opcode`, `duration_ns`, `start_ts`, `end_ts`) so you can find the
             slots that actually cost time, and chapter 11 maps a slot back to
             its NKI line. ## Working method 1. Measure or read the current
             profile before changing anything. 2. Form one hypothesis, name the
             slots it touches, and predict the effect. 3. Make the smallest edit
             that tests it. Check `semaphores.py`, then `verify.py`, then
             `profile.py`. 4. Write what you learned in `notes/` -- iterations
             are isolated and `notes/` is the only thing that survives,
             including after a rejected iteration. Record failures too: a wait
             that cannot be removed is worth as much as a win. 5. If an edit is
             wrong, `cp baseline/kernel.neff kernel.neff` and start again. A
             0.1% change is noise on this kernel; do not claim a win below about
             0.5% without repeating the measurement.
